# NIZAM - environment template for the `backup` service. TEMPLATE ONLY. NOTHING HERE IS EXECUTED.
#
# Owning contract: PFOS 12 - Two-Agent VPS Deployment & Operations, §7.1 (consistent snapshot,
#   public-key encryption whose private half is absent from the host, shred the plaintext, narrow
#   per-file storage grant, verified upload, bounded retention), §7.5 (the cadence is a
#   configuration value, not a hope), §3.2.2 (the one service permitted a cross-store view, and
#   every store mount read-only), §3.2.7 (one environment file per service), §8.2 (the coarse halt),
#   §7.6 (this path).
# Spec: .kiro/specs/06-two-agent-vps/ - task 7.3. Contract 12, phase 7. Requirements R6, R20, R23,
#   R24.
# Steering: .kiro/steering/two-agent-vps.md §0b (the backup public key is a deployment particular
#   too), §2 (writing this file is permitted, running it is not), §7 (placeholders only, AC09);
#   drive-db.md (the per-file storage scope, never the full scope).
#
# Companion: ops/docker-compose.yml declares this file as this service's single env_file, under the
#   placeholder <BACKUP_ENV_PATH>, holds all three stores read-only, and puts this service on its
#   own egress network with no other member. Audited by src/server/ops/envTemplates.ts.
#
# HOW TO READ AN ENTRY. Each assignment is preceded by exactly three annotation lines:
#   what:    one line saying what the value is
#   gate:    which human gate in ops/GATE_REGISTER.md supplies it - G1..G8, or `operator` for a
#            value the operator simply chooses, or `build` for a path to an artefact the build emits
#   secret:  yes if disclosing the value would let someone reach or impersonate this deployment
#
# EVERY VALUE IS AN <ANGLE_BRACKET> PLACEHOLDER WHOSE NAME IS ITS OWN ENTRY NAME. In particular the
# recipient key below is a placeholder even though it is the PUBLIC half: steering §0b lists it among
# the values that never appear in a tracked file, because a reader who has it knows which archives
# were written for this deployment.
#
# THE ONE THING THIS FILE MUST NEVER CONTAIN IS THE PRIVATE HALF OF THE BACKUP KEY. Gate G8 generates
# the keypair on the operator machine and moves the private half to an off-host store. That is what
# makes §7.1's guarantee true rather than merely stated: this host can CREATE a backup it cannot
# READ, so a host compromise yields ciphertext and nothing else. A private key here would void the
# guarantee retroactively, for every archive ever taken.

# ---- the snapshot and the shred (contract 12 §7.1.1, §7.1.3) ----------------------------------

# what: scratch directory for the snapshot-encrypt-shred sequence; never a store, and never kept
# gate: G1
# secret: no
BACKUP_WORK_DIR=<BACKUP_WORK_DIR>

# what: how often a backup runs; the recovery objective is stated in terms of this cadence
# gate: operator
# secret: no
BACKUP_SCHEDULE=<BACKUP_SCHEDULE>

# ---- encryption to a recipient whose private half is off the host (§7.1.2, gate G8) -----------

# what: the PUBLIC recipient key each snapshot is encrypted to; the private half is never here
# gate: G8
# secret: no
AGE_PUBLIC_KEY=<AGE_PUBLIC_KEY>

# what: which public-key encryption tool is used, with the documented fallback as the alternative
# gate: operator
# secret: no
BACKUP_ENCRYPTION_SCHEME=<BACKUP_ENCRYPTION_SCHEME>

# ---- the narrow storage grant and bounded retention (§7.1, gate G5) --------------------------

# what: how many encrypted artefacts are retained before the oldest is dropped
# gate: operator
# secret: no
BACKUP_RETAIN_COUNT=<BACKUP_RETAIN_COUNT>

# what: an opaque reference to the destination folder the grant may write to and nothing else
# gate: G5
# secret: no
BACKUP_FOLDER_REF=<BACKUP_FOLDER_REF>

# what: the long-lived grant that refreshes an access token without an interactive prompt
# gate: G5
# secret: yes
DRIVE_REFRESH_TOKEN=<DRIVE_REFRESH_TOKEN>

# what: the client identifier the grant was issued to
# gate: G5
# secret: no
GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=<GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID>

# what: the client credential the grant was issued with
# gate: G5
# secret: yes
GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET=<GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET>

# what: the storage provider's documented token endpoint, resolved from its own documentation
# gate: operator
# secret: no
STORAGE_TOKEN_URL=<STORAGE_TOKEN_URL>

# ---- the kill switch, both forms (contract 12 §8.2) -------------------------------------------

# what: the sentinel path checked before a run begins; present means stop, and it is never cached
# gate: G1
# secret: no
KILL_SENTINEL_PATH=<KILL_SENTINEL_PATH>

# what: the coarse halt; 1 halts the backup service, 0 does not. Restart-scoped, not a panic stop
# gate: operator
# secret: no
NIZAM_KILL_ALL=<NIZAM_KILL_ALL>

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# What is deliberately absent, and why absence is the mechanism rather than a gap
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# 1. NO PRIVATE KEY, NO IDENTITY FILE, NO PASSPHRASE. See the header. Gate G8's whole verification
#    line is that a search of the host for private key material returns nothing, and the empty
#    output is the gate.
#
# 2. NO AGENT SECRET. No bot token, no model key, no webhook secret. §7.1's closing rule is that no
#    key, token, or environment file is ever included in a backup payload - backups contain data,
#    and secrets are re-provisioned rather than restored (§7.5's rebuild path). A service that held
#    an agent's secret in order to back it up would be the exception that rule exists to refuse.
#
# 3. NO STORE PATH. ops/docker-compose.yml mounts all three stores read-only at fixed targets in this
#    container, so the paths are topology, not configuration. Naming them here would create a second
#    place a read-only mount could be described as writable.
#
# 4. NO WRITE ACCESS TO ANYTHING IT READS, EXPRESSIBLE OR OTHERWISE. Every store mount is read-only
#    (§3.2.2), the sentinel is read-only, and the only writable path is the scratch directory above.
#    There is no entry that could turn a read-only view into a writable one.
#
# 5. NO FULL-STORAGE SCOPE, AND NO SERVICE IDENTITY. The grant is a per-file user grant (drive-db.md,
#    harness check AC08). §7.1 records the documented trap: a service identity has no personal
#    storage quota, so its uploads fail or orphan. There is no entry here for a service identity
#    because substituting one is not a configuration choice, it is a broken backup path.
